                    "line": 9,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Andrew Geissler",
                        "email": "geissonator@yahoo.com",
                        "username": "geissonator"
                    },
                    "message": "nit: Still not clear to me in this message the overall design/purpose. I'd like it mentioned that an Active BMC is syncing over the persistent data files to the passive BMC, and we are then utilizing this new function to tell the service config manager on the passive BMC to reload the changed data and apply it to the passive BMC. In this way we ensure the passive BMC is enabling/disabling the same services as the Active BMC."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 9,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Riya Sabu",
                        "email": "riya.sabu461@gmail.com",
                        "username": "riya461"
                    },
                    "message": "Okay, I have updated the commit message to include more details as mentioned."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 9,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Andrew Geissler",
                        "email": "geissonator@yahoo.com",
                        "username": "geissonator"
                    },
                    "message": "Acknowledged"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 76,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Andrew Geissler",
                        "email": "geissonator@yahoo.com",
                        "username": "geissonator"
                    },
                    "message": "Why did the bmcweb server start back up here? Oh, probably socket activation? Could you also set Masked to true do it doesn't start back up automatically?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 76,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Riya Sabu",
                        "email": "riya.sabu461@gmail.com",
                        "username": "riya461"
                    },
                    "message": "Added a new test case scenario with Masked set to true, Enabling bmc service.\nIn case of the tested scenario, is this the expected behavior ?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 76,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Andrew Geissler",
                        "email": "geissonator@yahoo.com",
                        "username": "geissonator"
                    },
                    "message": "Hmm, I wanted the masked added to this test (i.e. Enabled:false and Masked:true) so that we don't see bmcweb start back up in the test data. I just find it confusing that we're saying we want to disable bmcweb but then the journal shows it starting back up. I think it's due to its socket activation so masking it will prevent it from starting back up and then the journal from the test would make sense. If it's not the socket activation then your test shows we have a bug because bmcweb should not be starting back up."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 76,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Riya Sabu",
                        "email": "riya.sabu461@gmail.com",
                        "username": "riya461"
                    },
                    "message": "From the complete traces, I believe it is due to socket activation as you mentioned. In the journal traces of disabling service when masked is set to true, the DBus property is updated, but service is not restarted. Updated the test scenario."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 76,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Riya Sabu",
                        "email": "riya.sabu461@gmail.com",
                        "username": "riya461"
                    },
                    "message": "Done"
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/srvcfg_manager.cpp",
                    "line": 438,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Andrew Geissler",
                        "email": "geissonator@yahoo.com",
                        "username": "geissonator"
                    },
                    "message": "I'm not sure why the \"Unit\" was added here? As we're dealing with systemd a lot in this code, a unit means something (a basic systemd service/target/socket). This function is really enabling/disabling/masking systemd units. So I think the more generic \"reloadServiceConfig()\" made more sense. If you want more, something like \"reloadServicecConfigData()\"? But I'd prefer we not put Unit on it."
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/srvcfg_manager.cpp",
                    "line": 438,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Riya Sabu",
                        "email": "riya.sabu461@gmail.com",
                        "username": "riya461"
                    },
                    "message": "Yes, I got that it can be misleading naming. Updated the function name."
                },
